One common way Java APIs can lead to vulnerabilities is through improper handling of user input when interacting with the system shell, which can lead to Command Injection.

```java
import java.io.*;

public class CommandInjectionExample {
    public void executeSystemCommand(String userInput) throws IOException {
        // Vulnerable: Directly concatenating user input into a shell command 
        // allows an attacker to inject additional commands using characters like ';' or '&'.
        String command = "ping -c 1 " + userInput;

        // Running the command through a shell interpreter
        Process process = Runtime.getRuntime().exec(new String[]{"/bin/sh", "-c", command});

        try (BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(process.getInputStream()))) {
            String line;
            while ((line = reader.readLine()) != null) {
                System.out.println(line);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

